# Standalone Web UI demo inspired by https://nsmat.github.io/lefts/
# No include, fixture, filesystem, Python, sklearn, or network access is used.

def u:page_require(condition,message) { "Stop with a named error when one demo invariant fails."; if condition { ok(1) } else { err(message) } }
def u:page_dot4(w,a,b,c) { "Predict from bias plus three normalized tabular features."; take(w,0,0)+take(w,0,1)*a+take(w,0,2)*b+take(w,0,3)*c }
def u:page_fit_ridge(cutoff,months,temp,humidity,hour,demand,steps,learning_rate,lambda,max_work) { "Fit one independent teaching Ridge model using rows strictly before cutoff.";
  rows=0; i=0; while lt(i,tally(months)) { if lt(take(months,0,i),cutoff) { rows=rows+1 } else { 0 }; i=i+1 };
  if lt(rows,1)+gt(rows*steps,max_work) { err("rolling Ridge fit has no history or exceeds work budget") } else {
    w=[0,0,0,0]; step=0;
    while lt(step,steps) { gradient=[0,0,0,0]; i=0; while lt(i,tally(months)) { if lt(take(months,0,i),cutoff) { a=take(temp,0,i); h=take(humidity,0,i); t=take(hour,0,i); error=u:page_dot4(w,a,h,t)-take(demand,0,i); gradient=gradient+[error,error*a,error*h,error*t] } else { 0 }; i=i+1 }; gradient=gradient*(2/rows)+[0,2*lambda*take(w,0,1),2*lambda*take(w,0,2),2*lambda*take(w,0,3)]; w=w-learning_rate*gradient; step=step+1 };
    ok({weights: w,training_rows: rows,cutoff_month: cutoff})
  }
}
def u:page_rolling_lift(months,temp,humidity,hour,demand,b) { "Lift the Ridge leaf over months 1 through 12 and route each row to its matching model.";
  weights=[]; training_rows=[]; predictions=[]; model_month=1;
  while lt(model_month,13) { fitted=u:page_fit_ridge(model_month,months,temp,humidity,hour,demand,b.steps,b.learning_rate,b.lambda,b.max_fit_work)?; weights=concat(weights,fitted.weights); training_rows=concat(training_rows,fitted.training_rows); row=model_month; prediction=u:page_dot4(fitted.weights,take(temp,0,row),take(humidity,0,row),take(hour,0,row)); predictions=concat(predictions,prediction); model_month=model_month+1 };
  targets=[]; i=1; while lt(i,13) { targets=concat(targets,take(demand,0,i)); i=i+1 }; errors=predictions-targets; ok({model_months: iota(12)+1,training_rows: training_rows,weights: reshape(weights,[12,4]),predictions: predictions,targets: targets,mse: reduce_add(errors*errors)/12,routing:"prediction cell m uses only model m, trained on rows whose month is less than m",aggregation:"coalesce in month order; exactly one model supplies each cell"})
}

# Month 0 is prior history, so even the January model has one earlier row.
months=iota(13);
temp=[0.10,0.18,0.25,0.34,0.45,0.58,0.72,0.80,0.75,0.62,0.47,0.31,0.20];
humidity=[0.82,0.78,0.72,0.65,0.58,0.50,0.43,0.39,0.42,0.51,0.62,0.72,0.79];
hour=[0.20,0.30,0.40,0.50,0.60,0.70,0.80,0.90,0.75,0.60,0.45,0.35,0.25];
demand=5+3*temp-2*humidity+1.5*hour;
budgets={steps:240,learning_rate:0.04,lambda:0.05,max_fit_work:40000,max_models:12,max_rows:13};
result=u:page_rolling_lift(months,temp,humidity,hour,demand,budgets)?;
u:page_require(equal(result.model_months,iota(12)+1),"expected twelve monthly model identities")?;
u:page_require(equal(result.training_rows,iota(12)+1),"each monthly model must use all and only earlier rows")?;
u:page_require(equal(shape(result.weights),[12,4]),"expected one four-parameter Ridge fit per month")?;
u:page_require(eq(tally(result.predictions),12)*eq(tally(result.targets),12),"coalesced prediction shape mismatch")?;
u:page_require(eq(result.mse,result.mse),"rolling MSE must be finite")?;

print("DEMO: standalone LEFTS landing-page equivalent for the sw-MLPL Web UI");
print("=== WHAT YOU ARE ABOUT TO SEE ===");
print("WHAT WILL COME: twelve leakage-safe monthly retrains, their routed predictions, and an inspectable workflow tree");
print("DOMAIN: forecast monthly demand from temperature, humidity, and hour-like activity");
print("The practical problem: demand changes with weather and activity, and the relationship may drift over the year. A model used in January cannot learn from July. We therefore simulate the decision available at each month: train only on the past, predict that month, then move forward.");
print("Why not fit once on the whole year? That would let future observations influence earlier predictions and make the backtest look better than a deployable forecast. Expanding-window retraining preserves time order while letting later models learn from more history.");
print("Scenario: mirror LEFTS's rolling monthly retrain example with twelve independent Ridge-like tabular models. Model m trains on every row before month m, predicts month m only, and the twelve routed predictions are coalesced in calendar order.");
print("Native boundary: sw-MLPL supplies numeric arrays, records, loops, reshape, and reductions. This single MLPL file owns generated tabular data, Ridge gradient descent, expanding-window filters, twelve fits, routing, aggregation, checks, and explanation.");
print("Budgets: 12 models, 13 tiny rows including prior history, 240 gradient steps per fit, at most 40000 row-steps, four parameters per model, and no I/O or external dependencies.");
print("=== KEY CONCEPTS ===");
print("Leaf: one ordinary Ridge-like learner with fit and predict behavior. It learns a bias and weights for temperature, humidity, and activity.");
print("KEY CONCEPT: Lift turns one Ridge leaf into twelve independently fitted monthly computations");
print("Train filter: monthly model m sees rows whose month is strictly less than m. Its training count therefore grows from 1 to 12.");
print("Test routing: a row from month m receives a prediction only from model m; the other eleven models do not contribute to that cell.");
print("Coalesce: the twelve non-overlapping monthly outputs are assembled into one calendar-ordered prediction vector.");
print("Inspectability: before interpreting metrics, we can print the workflow shape and then inspect every monthly fit, routing count, prediction, and target.");
print("=== WORKFLOW BEFORE EXECUTION ===");
print("Equivalent workflow tree:");
print("Lift 'monthly_retrain' (12 models): [month 1, ..., month 12] => coalesce -> monthly_retrain");
print("    `-- Leaf 'ridge' (bias + temp + humidity + hour)");
print("Generated feature rows (month 0 is prior history):"); print({month: months,temp: temp,humidity: humidity,hour: hour,demand: demand});
print("Expanding training-row count for each independent monthly model:"); print(result.training_rows);
print("Fitted rows are [bias, temp, humidity, hour], one row per monthly model:"); print(result.weights);
print("Each month receives only its matching model's prediction:"); print({months: result.model_months,predictions: result.predictions,actual_demand: result.targets,mse: result.mse});
print("Output explanation: early models have little history and less stable coefficients; later models see progressively more prior months. The coalesced vector is out-of-time because no model trains on its own month or a future month.");
print("The MSE summarizes all twelve forecast errors, but the predictions and targets remain visible so the metric is explainable. This tiny dataset is intentionally educational: it demonstrates experimental structure, not production forecast quality.");
print("EQUIVALENCE structure=leaf+lift+expanding-train-filter+monthly-test-filter+coalesce");
print("MODELS fitted=12");
print("LEAKAGE current_or_future_rows_in_fit=0");
print("COMPATIBILITY python_lefts_api=no");
print("Interpretation: this reproduces the landing page's experiment structure and inspectable Lift-to-Leaf story in ordinary MLPL. It uses a tiny in-file gradient-descent Ridge analogue instead of sklearn Ridge and numeric months instead of Polars dates, so it is conceptually equivalent rather than an API port.");
print("=== WHAT WE SHOWED ===");
print("WHAT WE SHOWED: expanding history -> independent fits -> month routing -> coalesced out-of-time predictions");
print("1. One Leaf learner can be treated as a reusable computation description.");
print("2. Lift repeats that learner across twelve month contexts without mixing their fitted parameters.");
print("3. Train and test filters encode the time-causal experiment: past trains, matching month tests.");
print("4. Coalescing reconstructs one useful forecast series from twelve disjoint model outputs.");
print("5. The printed tree, fitted weights, row counts, predictions, targets, and MSE make the transformed workflow inspectable rather than opaque.");
print("Takeaway: the interesting LEFTS idea is closure and composition around fit/predict computations. This standalone MLPL version shows that useful structure directly, without adding LEFTS-specific syntax or hiding the experiment in a library call.");
ok(1)
